WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:06.437
Let's take a look at this clay tablet.

00:00:06.904 --> 00:00:08.506
It might not look like much,

00:00:08.539 --> 00:00:11.542
but it's actually some of the oldest
mathematics we have.

00:00:11.575 --> 00:00:15.613
It’s a 4,000-year-old message in a bottle
from ancient Babylon --

00:00:15.646 --> 00:00:18.182
a precursor to the quadratic equation.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:20.217
And for four millennia,

00:00:20.217 --> 00:00:23.120
people have been doing math
basically the same way.

00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:25.856
Someone will have a brilliant idea,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.527
they’ll write it down,
and their peers will discuss and check it.

00:00:30.261 --> 00:00:35.833
It’s a process built on creativity,
communication and, most importantly,

00:00:35.866 --> 00:00:37.468
trust between people.

00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:42.673
And what might seem like a humble
or simple process is anything but.

00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:44.542
It's not just been successful.

00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:48.746
It's been, as the physicist
Eugene Wigner famously put it,

00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:50.614
unreasonably effective.

00:00:50.648 --> 00:00:54.585
Wigner was pondering and trying
to unravel a deep mystery.

00:00:54.585 --> 00:00:57.922
Why should the abstract, creative,

00:00:57.955 --> 00:01:01.892
and often bizarre ideas that spring
from a mathematician's imagination

00:01:01.926 --> 00:01:06.297
so often be the perfect language
with which we understand the universe?

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.333
Why should the strange laws
of non-Euclidean geometry,

00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:12.103
which were originally conceived of
as a thought experiment

00:01:12.103 --> 00:01:13.504
in the 19th century,

00:01:13.537 --> 00:01:15.306
turn out to be the exact mathematics

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:17.675
that Einstein needed
for general relativity?

00:01:17.708 --> 00:01:20.411
Why should the esoteric
math of group theory,

00:01:20.444 --> 00:01:24.281
which was originally designed to study
the abstract nature of symmetry,

00:01:24.315 --> 00:01:28.252
be fundamental to understanding
everything from particle physics

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:29.920
to the patterns in crystals?

00:01:30.554 --> 00:01:34.225
Well, there's no logical reason
it has to be this way.

00:01:34.825 --> 00:01:37.094
This strange connection

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:41.165
between pure mathematical
thought and the real world

00:01:41.198 --> 00:01:44.768
has actually been the invisible engine
driving human progress.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.807
Every piece of technology
that defines our lives

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.009
was ignited with a mathematical spark.

00:01:52.443 --> 00:01:54.612
If you take the device in your phone,

00:01:54.645 --> 00:01:57.882
its brain is based on the quantum
mechanics of semiconductors.

00:01:57.915 --> 00:02:00.451
And that's a theory
built on linear algebra

00:02:00.484 --> 00:02:02.219
and complex numbers.

00:02:02.253 --> 00:02:04.788
The wireless signals that get data to it,

00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:08.259
they're just a concrete
manifestation of Maxwell's equations.

00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:13.197
And finally, the security
that protects your data online

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.099
is based on number theory,

00:02:15.132 --> 00:02:18.669
which for a long time was truly
considered the most pure

00:02:18.702 --> 00:02:22.072
and least applicable
possible branch of mathematics.

00:02:22.806 --> 00:02:26.410
And now it safeguards trillions of dollars
in the global economy.

00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:28.612
And now we come to AI.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.751
Modern AI is not just built with math,
it's forged from it.

00:02:34.218 --> 00:02:38.289
A neural network is just a monumental
structure of applied mathematics.

00:02:38.322 --> 00:02:40.024
And when AIs learn,

00:02:40.057 --> 00:02:42.326
they're using the tools of calculus

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:44.662
to navigate vast landscapes
of possibilities

00:02:44.695 --> 00:02:46.430
with billions of dimensions.

00:02:46.931 --> 00:02:49.066
So AI is, in its soul,

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:52.503
a mathematical idea
that's given life through computation.

00:02:53.737 --> 00:02:58.776
So we agree that math is the foundation
that modern civilization is based on.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.813
But that foundation is starting
to show some signs of strain.

00:03:03.214 --> 00:03:06.016
The very process of human-led discovery

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.220
that's gotten us to this point
is nearing a breaking point,

00:03:09.253 --> 00:03:11.388
buckling under the weight
of its own success.

00:03:11.422 --> 00:03:15.226
And now AI, which is one
of mathematics’ greatest creations,

00:03:15.259 --> 00:03:17.428
is accelerating us towards
that breaking point

00:03:17.461 --> 00:03:19.363
faster than the world's ready for.

00:03:19.396 --> 00:03:21.398
So let's just look at some evidence.

00:03:21.966 --> 00:03:23.867
Consider the Poincaré conjecture.

00:03:24.335 --> 00:03:26.103
This is a legendary problem.

00:03:26.136 --> 00:03:27.438
It's a fundamental question

00:03:27.471 --> 00:03:30.107
about the nature
of three-dimensional shapes

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.876
originally posed in 1904.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.878
And for nearly a century,

00:03:34.912 --> 00:03:38.249
it stood as an unconquered
Everest of mathematics.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:40.851
Until in 2002,

00:03:40.884 --> 00:03:42.987
a Russian mathematician
working in isolation

00:03:42.987 --> 00:03:44.688
named Grigori Perelman

00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:48.325
posted a series of three short,
cryptic papers online.

00:03:48.325 --> 00:03:50.628
He didn’t bother submitting
them to a journal --

00:03:50.628 --> 00:03:52.930
he just put them on the internet
and walked away.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.666
His fellow mathematicians
had to stop what they were doing

00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:57.434
and try to decipher it.

00:03:57.434 --> 00:04:02.506
And several teams working independently
of the best of colleges in the world,

00:04:02.506 --> 00:04:05.776
took the next four years
to try to unpack the arguments,

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:07.778
fill in the logical gaps

00:04:07.811 --> 00:04:10.581
and eventually, at the end,
after they really reviewed it,

00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:11.949
declare that yes, he did it.

00:04:11.982 --> 00:04:13.951
He proved the Poincaré conjecture.

00:04:14.385 --> 00:04:15.653
But that's interesting

00:04:15.686 --> 00:04:19.990
because it took one person
to write a proof

00:04:20.024 --> 00:04:24.395
and a global, multi-year
intellectual mobilization to check it.

00:04:25.429 --> 00:04:28.332
And that's in the best case,
when the proof is correct.

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:32.169
Consider Andrew Wiles's
proof of Fermat's Last Theorem.

00:04:32.202 --> 00:04:36.907
With the electrifying announcement in 1993
in Cambridge, the world celebrated.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.210
But during the peer-review
process, deep in it,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.579
a single thread was found out of place

00:04:42.613 --> 00:04:45.182
in that magnificent tapestry of a proof,

00:04:45.215 --> 00:04:46.850
and when we started to pull on it,

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:48.285
the proof started to unravel.

00:04:48.285 --> 00:04:50.020
And this wasn't a small mistake.

00:04:50.587 --> 00:04:55.993
Andrew Wiles and his collaborator
Richard Taylor took two years of heroic,

00:04:56.026 --> 00:04:58.162
secret effort to try to fix it.

00:04:58.495 --> 00:05:02.433
And that effort included
some insights that Andrew Wiles said

00:05:02.466 --> 00:05:04.501
were among the most important in his life.

00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:07.571
And that's before we throw
AI into the mix.

00:05:08.706 --> 00:05:10.140
Two short years ago,

00:05:10.174 --> 00:05:15.212
AI could barely solve entry-level
high school math-contest problems.

00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:17.681
They were very clever, but brittle.

00:05:18.582 --> 00:05:20.751
Now, in 2025,

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:22.486
they can compete with the best of us

00:05:22.519 --> 00:05:24.588
at the International Math Olympiad,

00:05:24.621 --> 00:05:27.424
which is the premier
precollege math competition.

00:05:28.258 --> 00:05:30.461
But the interesting bit is the following.

00:05:30.894 --> 00:05:35.999
The AI might work for four hours
and produce a purported solution,

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:40.304
which takes an expert human mathematician
maybe up to an hour to check.

00:05:41.338 --> 00:05:44.308
And we all know the exponential
trend that AI is on.

00:05:44.341 --> 00:05:49.079
So we can expect it’s not going
to be one proof in an afternoon --

00:05:49.113 --> 00:05:51.248
it’s going to be a thousand pretty soon.

00:05:51.815 --> 00:05:55.185
And they're not going to be attempts
to solve math-contest problems.

00:05:55.219 --> 00:05:57.621
They're going to be attacks
on the most fundamental

00:05:57.654 --> 00:05:59.690
and important questions of the day,

00:05:59.723 --> 00:06:01.492
whether it's the Riemann hypothesis,

00:06:01.492 --> 00:06:04.161
Navier-Stokes or P versus NP,

00:06:04.161 --> 00:06:05.729
just to pick a few.

00:06:05.763 --> 00:06:08.665
We simply don't have the human bandwidth

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:10.401
to review all these proofs.

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:12.536
There's only a couple
thousand mathematicians

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:15.506
that are qualified to do it,
and they already have day jobs.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:17.808
And it's not just
a verification bottleneck.

00:06:17.841 --> 00:06:20.144
The very process
by which we train these AIs

00:06:20.177 --> 00:06:22.146
is taking the data off the internet,

00:06:22.179 --> 00:06:23.547
which is from humans,

00:06:23.547 --> 00:06:25.849
post-training them with human feedback,

00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:28.519
and so we're essentially
baking in the cognitive biases

00:06:28.552 --> 00:06:32.956
and the flawed reasoning of humans
into these future engines of discovery.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.160
So the conclusion
is in some sense obvious.

00:06:36.193 --> 00:06:39.596
Humans are becoming
the bottleneck of verification for AI.

00:06:40.097 --> 00:06:42.633
And now the question is,
where does that leave us?

00:06:42.666 --> 00:06:46.270
Is this the end of the road
for reliable mathematical discovery?

00:06:46.303 --> 00:06:49.173
Are we resigned to drowning
in a sea of unverified claims

00:06:49.206 --> 00:06:51.575
where we can't really tell
truth from fiction?

00:06:51.608 --> 00:06:53.710
And are we about to squander
the opportunity

00:06:53.744 --> 00:06:55.679
for AI to revolutionize math?

00:06:55.979 --> 00:06:57.848
Well, the good news is no.

00:06:58.315 --> 00:06:59.583
But it does mean it's time

00:06:59.616 --> 00:07:02.920
to upgrade the 4,000-year-old
operating system of math,

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:08.592
and move away from the imprecise
and ambiguous nature of human language,

00:07:08.592 --> 00:07:11.728
and towards a language
that computers can understand.

00:07:12.496 --> 00:07:15.466
The solution is formal mathematics.

00:07:16.834 --> 00:07:19.336
But before I tell you
how this futuristic idea works,

00:07:19.369 --> 00:07:23.140
we should first recognize that it has
a deep and fascinating history

00:07:23.173 --> 00:07:25.976
dating back to the 17th century,

00:07:26.009 --> 00:07:29.913
where a mathematician actually laid out
the road map with stunning foresight.

00:07:30.314 --> 00:07:31.982
Four hundred years ago,

00:07:32.015 --> 00:07:35.853
in a Europe torn by religious
and political conflict,

00:07:35.886 --> 00:07:39.456
a polymath named Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz

00:07:39.456 --> 00:07:41.792
had a vision of breathtaking ambition.

00:07:42.626 --> 00:07:46.597
He was a contemporary of Newton
and a cocreator of calculus,

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.465
but his dreams went far beyond that.

00:07:49.233 --> 00:07:52.836
He dreamed of something
called a universal characteristic,

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:56.440
which was a system for perfectly
encoding all scientific

00:07:56.473 --> 00:07:58.075
and philosophical thought.

00:07:58.675 --> 00:08:00.944
And the system had three parts.

00:08:01.578 --> 00:08:04.848
First, you need a perfect
logical language.

00:08:05.749 --> 00:08:09.920
Second, you need a grand encyclopedia
written in language

00:08:09.953 --> 00:08:12.489
that contains all verified human thought.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.424
And third,

00:08:14.458 --> 00:08:16.193
and this is the masterstroke,

00:08:16.226 --> 00:08:18.896
you need a so-called engine of reason,

00:08:18.929 --> 00:08:20.364
a system of mechanical rules

00:08:20.364 --> 00:08:24.601
by which you can automatically derive
new facts from that library

00:08:24.635 --> 00:08:27.638
as surely as a calculator
performs arithmetic.

00:08:27.671 --> 00:08:31.408
Now, Leibniz thought
this would revolutionize humanity.

00:08:31.742 --> 00:08:33.343
With a system like this,

00:08:33.377 --> 00:08:35.445
if two people had
an intellectual conflict,

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:38.215
they would resort to logic
and not rhetoric to resolve it.

00:08:38.215 --> 00:08:39.616
They would simply sit down,

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.619
say “calculemus” -- “let us calculate,”

00:08:42.619 --> 00:08:44.321
and get to the bottom of it.

00:08:44.354 --> 00:08:48.258
In some sense, it was meant to be
a universal calculator for truth.

00:08:48.859 --> 00:08:51.395
Now, Leibniz was a bit of an optimist.

00:08:51.395 --> 00:08:55.098
He thought this would take a small
group of people five years to build,

00:08:55.132 --> 00:08:57.935
and he was off by several centuries.

00:08:58.468 --> 00:09:00.671
But what I think is really remarkable

00:09:00.671 --> 00:09:02.406
is that in 2025,

00:09:02.406 --> 00:09:04.775
truly for the first time in history,

00:09:04.808 --> 00:09:08.445
it's actually possible to realize
this philosopher's dream.

00:09:08.478 --> 00:09:09.646
So what do we need?

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.716
Well, we need a perfect, logical language.

00:09:12.749 --> 00:09:14.151
Turns out we've got it.

00:09:14.184 --> 00:09:15.752
It's called Lean.

00:09:15.786 --> 00:09:17.854
Lean is a programming language,

00:09:17.888 --> 00:09:20.691
but it's also what's known
as a proof assistant.

00:09:20.724 --> 00:09:23.126
You can think of it
as a programming environment

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.028
for mathematical proofs,

00:09:25.028 --> 00:09:26.930
where it doesn't just give you feedback

00:09:26.964 --> 00:09:28.966
if you have a syntax error
here or there --

00:09:28.966 --> 00:09:31.935
it's actually looking at the core
of the mathematical argument

00:09:31.969 --> 00:09:34.705
and telling you if you have
any problems anywhere in it.

00:09:34.738 --> 00:09:35.973
Great.

00:09:35.973 --> 00:09:37.541
What's the second thing we need?

00:09:37.574 --> 00:09:39.176
We need the grand encyclopedia.

00:09:39.509 --> 00:09:41.511
Well, the good news is we've got that too.

00:09:41.545 --> 00:09:43.246
It's called Mathlib.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.415
Mathlib is an open-source project.

00:09:45.449 --> 00:09:48.719
It's about two million
lines of code in Lean,

00:09:48.719 --> 00:09:52.756
and it covers a lot of the undergraduate
and graduate math curriculum.

00:09:53.357 --> 00:09:57.461
You can think of it like
a Wikipedia for proven truth,

00:09:57.494 --> 00:10:01.498
where every edit is computationally
certified for correctness.

00:10:02.332 --> 00:10:04.134
OK, we've got the language,

00:10:04.134 --> 00:10:05.836
we've got the encyclopedia,

00:10:05.836 --> 00:10:07.771
what about the engine of reason?

00:10:08.138 --> 00:10:10.607
Well, we could try to have humans do it,

00:10:10.641 --> 00:10:12.776
but you've got to write a lot of Lean code

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:16.079
and the level of robotic precision
you need to write a formal proof

00:10:16.113 --> 00:10:19.716
is not something that human creativity
is so well suited for.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:22.152
And that's how we've come full-circle.

00:10:22.152 --> 00:10:26.256
It turns out that AI is the key
to making this whole thing work.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.093
In the future, AI is not just going
to be writing math papers in English

00:10:30.127 --> 00:10:31.662
for humans to read.

00:10:31.695 --> 00:10:34.331
They're going to be writing
math proofs in Lean

00:10:34.364 --> 00:10:35.932
for computers to check.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.770
And that is the fundamental key

00:10:39.803 --> 00:10:42.806
that makes it possible
to use Leibniz's vision

00:10:42.839 --> 00:10:45.909
to unlock the full potential
of AI in mathematics.

00:10:46.777 --> 00:10:50.580
Because when a math AI spits out a proof
in Lean of, let's say,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.315
the Riemann hypothesis,

00:10:52.349 --> 00:10:56.286
we're not going to need humans to go
through every single line of the proof

00:10:56.319 --> 00:10:57.654
in painstaking detail,

00:10:57.688 --> 00:10:58.922
check every single case,

00:10:58.955 --> 00:11:03.193
and understand the possibly strange
and alien logic of the proof

00:11:03.226 --> 00:11:04.628
just to see if it's correct.

00:11:04.628 --> 00:11:07.798
Instead, all we're going to do
is we're going to take those files,

00:11:07.798 --> 00:11:10.734
we're going to give them
to a Lean compiler, and if it builds,

00:11:10.767 --> 00:11:13.070
we can know with absolute
certainty it's correct.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:17.441
And this is what fundamentally
alters our relationship with AI.

00:11:18.041 --> 00:11:20.410
AI can now become a true collaborator,

00:11:20.444 --> 00:11:24.147
one whose word we don't have
to take on blind faith.

00:11:24.581 --> 00:11:29.553
We get to trade in the tedium of checking
for the creative joy of discovery.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.590
Humans get to use
our intuition and judgment,

00:11:33.623 --> 00:11:35.058
we ask the questions,

00:11:35.092 --> 00:11:38.662
we chart the course,
we propose the brilliant conjectures

00:11:38.695 --> 00:11:43.300
and then we delegate to AI
to explore the vast oceans of logic,

00:11:43.333 --> 00:11:44.634
to find the correct answer,

00:11:44.668 --> 00:11:47.804
and then a computer confirms
that we've gotten to the destination.

00:11:48.572 --> 00:11:49.740
And the amazing thing

00:11:49.773 --> 00:11:52.609
is that this isn't just some
far-off science-fiction dream.

00:11:52.642 --> 00:11:55.679
It turns out that at this year’s
International Math Olympiad,

00:11:55.712 --> 00:11:59.583
automated systems were able to find
solutions to five of the six problems

00:11:59.616 --> 00:12:03.487
in a way that computers could check
and require no human review whatsoever.

00:12:03.487 --> 00:12:06.490
And that’s enough to get a
gold-medal-level performance.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.759
So the transition is already happening.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:14.464
So are humans going to be
the bottleneck for math research?

00:12:15.031 --> 00:12:18.769
Well, the answer is yes,
but only if we refuse to change.

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:21.671
Only if we insist
on being the only thinkers

00:12:21.705 --> 00:12:23.507
and the only checkers.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.676
But if we're able to realize
this 400-year-old vision,

00:12:26.676 --> 00:12:28.478
we're not going to replace ourselves,

00:12:28.478 --> 00:12:30.080
we're going to elevate ourselves.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:32.382
We're going to put ourselves
in the driver's seat

00:12:32.415 --> 00:12:35.152
as the explorers, the architects
and the question askers.

00:12:35.152 --> 00:12:39.256
And that means that formal mathematics
is the key to this new era of discovery,

00:12:39.289 --> 00:12:43.593
based on the powerful and essential
partnership between human imagination

00:12:43.627 --> 00:12:45.796
and mathematical superintelligence.

00:12:47.097 --> 00:12:48.265
Thank you.

00:12:48.298 --> 00:12:51.334
(Applause)

